## Primitives in the hydra.core.lib.hashing module.
##
## Digests are computed with SHA-256 as defined in FIPS 180-4; results are deterministic and bit-identical across hosts.
## Hashing is pure: both primitives are ordinary total functions of their input bytes, and hashing never fails.
module hydra.core.lib.hashing

## Compute the SHA-256 digest of a sequence of bytes.
##
## sha256(bytes) returns the 32-byte SHA-256 digest of bytes. Total: hashing is pure and never fails.
## Pairs with hydra.core.lib.files.readFile, which returns raw bytes, to hash file contents.
##
## @availableSince 0.17
## @requirement matchesKnownAnswerVectors: sha256 returns the correct 32-byte SHA-256 digest for known-answer input vectors, including the empty byte sequence.
primitive sha256 : binary → binary

## Compute the SHA-256 digest of a sequence of bytes as a lowercase hex string.
##
## sha256Hex(bytes) returns the SHA-256 digest of bytes as a 64-character lowercase hexadecimal string. Equivalent to hex-encoding the output of sha256. Total: hashing is pure and never fails.
## sha256Hex(bytes) is the lowercase hexadecimal rendering of the 32 bytes of sha256(bytes); this equivalence is the specification.
## Pairs with hydra.core.lib.files.readFile, which returns raw bytes, to hash file contents.
##
## @availableSince 0.17
## @requirement matchesKnownAnswerVectors: sha256Hex returns the correct 64-character lowercase hexadecimal SHA-256 digest for known-answer input vectors, including the empty byte sequence and a multi-block input.
## @requirement handlesPaddingBoundariesCorrectly: sha256Hex returns the correct digest at every SHA-256 message-padding boundary length (55 bytes, the largest message whose padding still fits one 64-byte block; 56 bytes, which forces a second block; and 63 and 64 bytes, straddling the block size), guarding against a padding-logic regression that would only surface at these specific lengths.
primitive sha256Hex : binary → string